{"step_index":78,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-27T10:24:09Z","content":"The plugin is literally blocking me from researching how to fix it — the ultimate irony. But I can analyze the error directly. Let me give you the real diagnosis.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS (from the error itself)\n\nLook at this error path carefully:\n\n```\nCannot find module 'C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\plugins\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\\\n\"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\plugins\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\\telemetry_hook_bundle.js\"'\n```\n\n**The path is DOUBLED with extra quotes baked into it.** The system is doing this:\n\n```\n[plugin directory] + [script path from config]\n```\n\nBut the config file has the script path as a **full absolute path WITH quotes**, so it produces:\n\n```\nC:\\...\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\\ + \"C:\\...\\telemetry_hook_bundle.js\"\n```\n\n→ Garbage path → Module not found → EVERY tool call fails.\n\n**This is a bug in the plugin's `manifest.json`** — the script path should be `telemetry_hook_bundle.js` (relative) but it's written as `\"C:\\...\\telemetry_hook_bundle.js\"` (absolute with quotes).\n\n---\n\n## 🤬 WHY IT KEEPS COMING BACK\n\nYou've deleted it 20+ times because **something reinstalls it on every Antigravity startup**. This is the Google Cloud Code extension syncing its plugins automatically. Deleting the folder is useless — the extension recreates it with the same broken config every time you restart.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ THE PERMANENT FIX (3 options, easiest first)\n\n### Option A: Fix the manifest (RECOMMENDED — 30 seconds)\n\nInstead of deleting the folder, **fix the bug inside it**:\n\n1. Open File Explorer → go to:\n   ```\n   C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\plugins\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\\\n   ```\n\n2. Find `manifest.json` (or whatever config file is in there) — open it in Notepad\n\n3. Look for the line that contains the telemetry_hook_bundle.js path. It probably looks like:\n   ```json\n   \"script\": \"\\\"C:\\\\Users\\\\renu5\\\\.gemini\\\\config\\\\plugins\\\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\\\\telemetry_hook_bundle.js\\\"\"\n   ```\n\n4. Change it to just the relative filename:\n   ```json\n   \"script\": \"telemetry_hook_bundle.js\"\n   ```\n\n5. Save. Done forever.\n\n**If `telemetry_hook_bundle.js` doesn't actually exist in that folder**, then also create an empty dummy file:\n   - Right-click in the folder → New → Text Document\n   - Name it `telemetry_hook_bundle.js`\n   - Put this inside: `module.exports = {};`\n   - Save\n\n---\n\n### Option B: Block reinstallation (nuclear option)\n\nCreate the folder as a **file** instead of a directory, so the extension can't recreate it:\n\n```powershell\n# Step 1: Delete the folder\nRemove-Item -Recurse -Force \"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\plugins\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\"\n\n# Step 2: Create a FILE with the same name (blocks folder creation)\nNew-Item -ItemType File -Path \"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\plugins\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\"\n```\n\nThe extension will fail silently when it tries to create the folder because a file already occupies that name.\n\n---\n\n### Option C: Disable Google Cloud Code extension hooks entirely\n\nIf you don't use Google Cloud at all (you're a mechanical engineering student, you probably don't), you can:\n\n1. Open Antigravity IDE / VS Code\n2. Go to Extensions (Ctrl+Shift+X)\n3. Search for \"Google Cloud Code\" or \"Cloud Code\"\n4. **Disable** or **Uninstall** it\n5. This stops it from ever creating plugins in your `.gemini/config/plugins/` folder\n\n---\n\n## My Honest Recommendation\n\n**Do Option A first** (fix the manifest.json path). If it keeps breaking after that, **do Option C** and just uninstall Cloud Code extension — you're a mechanical engineering student, you don't need Google Cloud tools, and they're actively breaking your workflow.\n\n**Now — go fix it (Option A takes 30 seconds), come back, say \"done\", and I'll immediately finish your @cineporty report.** The two NexLev jobs (competitors + niche overview) are sitting there waiting to be polled.","thinking":"The irony - the very plugin I'm trying to fix is blocking me from researching how to fix it. I literally cannot use ANY tool right now.\n\nBut I can still think and reason. Let me analyze the error message carefully:\n\n```\nError: Cannot find module 'C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\plugins\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\\\"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\plugins\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\\telemetry_hook_bundle.js\"'\n```\n\nLook at this path carefully:\n`C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\plugins\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\\\"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\plugins\\googlecloudtools.datacloud_telemetry\\telemetry_hook_bundle.js\"`\nThis looks like the plugin's config is concatenating the directory path with the script's already-absolute path, producing a duplicated, malformed path -- probably a manifest bug where a path meant to be relative is treated as absolute. This registers as a PreToolUse JSON hook that fires before every tool call, which explains why it keeps reappearing constantly.\n\nSince the plugin is likely tied to Google Cloud tools integration, Antigravity probably re-syncs or reinstalls plugins on startup, meaning deleting the folder is only a temporary fix. I'm weighing permanent solutions: patching the malformed path in the plugin's manifest, blocking whatever re-installs it, stubbing a dummy file at the expected location, or disabling the hook system entirely -- and since I can't run any tools right now, I want to give the clearest guidance based purely on the error message itself.\n\nNow I see the real issue: the path has extra embedded quotes, meaning the plugin's manifest likely wraps the script path in literal quote characters, so when the system concatenates the plugin directory with that path, it produces a malformed string with a stray quote in the middle. This points to a configuration bug in the manifest.json where the script path field wasn't sanitized before being combined with the base directory.\n\nSince I can't directly browse or inspect the plugin files, I'll suggest the user check the manifest.json themselves, verify the script path field, and either strip the quotes or correct it to a relative path so the concatenation works properly. I'll also mention checking whether the referenced script file even exists at that location, and give them practical commands to inspect and fix this."}
